WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.534 --> 00:00:04.239
A CPU fills a blob one pixel at a time.

2
00:00:04.475 --> 00:00:08.089
Pure Python: twenty-four seconds
for the real image.

3
00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:10.289
Numba: one point three.

4
00:00:10.593 --> 00:00:14.446
The table holds the CPU time
for seventeen shapes.

5
00:00:14.607 --> 00:00:20.034
Every chapter now gets a column,
and a shape glows when the GPU wins.

6
00:00:20.367 --> 00:00:21.617
Chapter one.

7
00:00:22.045 --> 00:00:27.402
One block of two hundred fifty-six threads
floods the whole frontier at once,

8
00:00:27.609 --> 00:00:31.272
its queue in shared memory,
a spill tier behind it.

9
00:00:31.652 --> 00:00:37.160
One block is one of twenty-four SMs:
four percent of the GPU.

10
00:00:37.398 --> 00:00:41.008
It beats the CPU only
on the biggest shapes.

11
00:00:41.332 --> 00:00:46.713
Chapter two. Two blocks on two SMs
share one global queue,

12
00:00:46.808 --> 00:00:49.487
with a grid-wide barrier twice per level.

13
00:00:49.704 --> 00:00:52.741
Twice as fast as one block on big blobs.

14
00:00:52.933 --> 00:00:56.087
The small shapes still belong to the CPU.

15
00:00:56.441 --> 00:00:59.489
Chapter three. Any number of blocks.

16
00:00:59.915 --> 00:01:03.321
Every block takes the next pixels
from the same queue,

17
00:01:03.470 --> 00:01:07.658
and the hue shows who filled what:
ownership speckles.

18
00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:10.941
Eight blocks here,
forty-eight in the benchmark.

19
00:01:11.255 --> 00:01:13.389
Eight neighbors instead of four.

20
00:01:13.575 --> 00:01:18.695
The wave becomes a square and needs
half the levels, so half the barriers.

21
00:01:18.925 --> 00:01:22.398
On the big disk, eleven times the CPU.

22
00:01:22.739 --> 00:01:26.241
Chapter four. Two blobs in one launch:

23
00:01:26.423 --> 00:01:29.213
the label rides inside the queue entry.

24
00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:33.530
But on the real image every blob was
still its own launch,

25
00:01:33.756 --> 00:01:38.100
two and a half thousand launches,
and the GPU lost.

26
00:01:38.442 --> 00:01:41.485
Chapter five. No seeds given.

27
00:01:42.028 --> 00:01:47.005
Candidate waves start everywhere and
colliding waves merge in flight.

28
00:01:47.210 --> 00:01:52.234
Two and a half thousand blobs, one launch,
fifty-eight milliseconds.

29
00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:54.847
Now the real image glows too.

30
00:01:55.206 --> 00:01:58.692
Chapter six. Why move pixels at all?

31
00:01:59.156 --> 00:02:02.097
A red span in a row is one run:

32
00:02:02.303 --> 00:02:05.004
twenty-five times fewer things to touch.

33
00:02:05.290 --> 00:02:08.112
Seven plain launches, no barrier.

34
00:02:08.470 --> 00:02:11.767
Fifty-eight milliseconds becomes
one and a half.

35
00:02:12.097 --> 00:02:15.313
Twenty-four seconds to
one and a half milliseconds.

36
00:02:15.405 --> 00:02:17.725
Sixteen thousand times.

37
00:02:17.992 --> 00:02:19.804
Not a smarter algorithm.

38
00:02:19.984 --> 00:02:21.908
A better representation.

